(window.LADDER_CARDS["30a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30a1.1","q":"What is a cover of a set A, what is the outer size m*(A), and what does the cover-total technique say?","a":"a cover is countably many open intervals that together hold A; m*(A) is the greatest lower bound of the totals of their lengths, over all covers; and the outer size of an interval is its length","w":["a cover is one interval that holds A; m*(A) is its length; every set has the outer size of the smallest interval round it","a cover is finitely many intervals inside A; m*(A) is the largest total; an interval has outer size 0","a cover is any set holding A; m*(A) is the number of its points; an interval has endless outer size"],"why":"Every cover of [u, v] totals at least v − u."},
 {"u":"30a2.1","q":"What does the outer-adds-at-most technique say, and why does every countable set have outer size 0?","a":"the outer size of a union of countably many sets is at most the sum of their outer sizes; each point has outer size 0, so a countable set, a countable union of points, has outer size at most 0 + 0 + ⋯","w":["the outer size of a union equals the sum of the outer sizes; countable sets have outer size 1","the outer size of a union is at least the sum; countable sets have outer size 0 because they are finite","the technique holds for two sets only; countable sets have no outer size"],"why":"A set can be everywhere and still take up no room."},
 {"u":"30a3.1","q":"What is A + t, and what does the shift-keeps technique say, and why?","a":"A + t is A with every point slid by t; m*(A + t) = m*(A), because sliding a cover of A gives a cover of A + t with the same lengths, and back again","w":["A + t is A with t added as a new point; its outer size grows by 0","A + t is A stretched by t; m*(A + t) = t · m*(A)","A + t is A slid by t; its outer size may change, since covers do not slide"],"why":"The same totals, so the same least total."},
 {"u":"30a4.1","q":"What three things would a size on the line need, what does the no-size-set technique say, and where does its proof spend choice?","a":"it would give intervals their lengths, be kept by sliding, and add over countably many separate pieces; if choice is true, some set in [0, 1] can be given no such size; choice is spent in building that set V, one member picked from each family of reals that differ by fractions","w":["it would need only to give intervals their lengths; every set then has a size; choice is not used","it would need to add over any number of pieces; no set has a size; choice is spent in counting the fractions","it would need the three things; the no-size set is the fractions; choice picks the first fraction"],"why":"Choice says V exists; it names no member of it."},
 {"u":"30a6.1","q":"What is wrong with “the fractions sit in every interval, so they have size 1 in [0, 1]”?","a":"being everywhere is not taking up room: the fractions are countable, so they can be covered by intervals of total length as small as you like, and their outer size is 0","w":["the fractions have size ½, since half the reals are fractions","the fractions have no outer size, being scattered","nothing; a set in every interval fills [0, 1]"],"why":"Cover the kth fraction by an interval of length ε/2ᵏ."},
 {"u":"30a5.1","q":"What is λ, and over which splits does it add? Why can the same thing not happen to the length of [u, v] on the line?","a":"the total length of a set made of finitely many intervals of fractions of [0, 1]; it adds over finitely many separate pieces, and not over the countable split of F into single fractions; on the line a cover of [u, v] by open intervals has total at least v − u, by a least upper bound","w":["the outer size of a set of fractions; it adds over every split; because [u, v] has no fractions in it","the number of fractions in a set; it adds over countable splits only; because the line is not countable","the length of the shortest interval in the set; it adds over no split; because lengths on the line can be endless"],"why":"30a5: every single fraction has λ = 0 and F has λ = 1, and F is a countable union of single fractions."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30b1.1","q":"What is the split test, when is a set sized, and which half of the test needs checking, and why?","a":"A is sized when it splits every set E cleanly, m*(E) = m*(E ∩ A) + m*(E ∖ A); only ≤ m*(E) on the right needs checking, since the other half is the outer-adds-at-most technique; and then the outside of A is sized too","w":["A is sized when m*(A) is finite; nothing needs checking","A is sized when it splits every interval E; both halves need checking separately","A is sized when m*(E ∩ A) ≤ m*(E) for every E; that half holds for every set, so every set is sized"],"why":"A set of outer size 0 is sized."},
 {"u":"30b2.1","q":"What does the sized-family technique say, and how does the proof reach countably many sets from two?","a":"if A₁, A₂, … are sized, so is their union, and so is the outside of any one of them; the proof does two sets, then finitely many by induction, makes them separate by taking each one’s part outside those before it, and lets n grow","w":["a union of any number of sized sets is sized; the proof takes one point at a time","a union of two sized sets is sized, but not of countably many","a union of sized sets is sized only when they are separate; the proof stops at finitely many"],"why":"Countably many, not any number: every set is a union of its single points."},
 {"u":"30b3.1","q":"What does the countable-adding technique say, and what are the books that size keeps?","a":"if A₁, A₂, … are sized and separate, m of their union is m(A₁) + m(A₂) + ⋯; size keeps exact books over countably many separate pieces, and a set that would break the books is left out of them","w":["m of a union is at most the sum, even for separate sized sets; the books are estimates","m adds over any number of separate pieces; no set is left out","m adds over finitely many pieces only; the books stop there"],"why":"The fractions in [0, 1] total 0, leaving 1 for the rest."},
 {"u":"30b4.1","q":"What does the intervals-sized technique say, how does its proof open, and which sets are the line’s sizes?","a":"every interval is sized, and its size is its length; the proof opens with a cover of E and cuts each of its intervals at the ends of the interval being tested, so the lengths add; the line’s sizes are the sized sets with their size m, among them every open and every closed set","w":["only intervals are sized; the proof opens with choice; the line’s sizes are the lengths of intervals","every interval is sized with size 0; the proof opens by sliding; the line’s sizes are the countable sets","every set is sized; the proof opens with the no-size set; the line’s sizes are all sets"],"why":"Every open set is a countable union of intervals with fraction ends."},
 {"u":"30b5.1","q":"What is wrong with “every set passes the split test”?","a":"if choice is true the set V of 30a4 fails it: were V sized, the three demands would give it a size, and it can have none","w":["no set passes the split test","only countable sets fail it","nothing; the split test is true of every set by outer-adds-at-most"],"why":"The ledger leaves V out rather than let the books fail."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30c1.1","q":"What is a step function, what is its total, and what does the step-total technique say?","a":"a function taking finitely many values, each on a sized set; its total is the sum of each value times the size of its set; the total does not depend on how the function is written, totals add, and a step function below another has a total no larger","w":["a function with jumps; its total is its largest value; totals multiply","a function on intervals only; its total is the sum of its values; the total depends on the cutting","a continuous function; its total is its area; a larger function may have a smaller total"],"why":"Compare on the common pieces."},
 {"u":"30c2.1","q":"What is a sized function, and what does the height-slices technique say?","a":"a function each of whose height sets, where f is above a, is sized; continuous functions are sized, and so are sums, products and the least upper bound of a list of sized functions","w":["a bounded function; only continuous functions are sized","a function with a finite total; sums of sized functions need not be sized","a function with sized values; every function is sized"],"why":"10d’s q, 1 on the fractions and 0 elsewhere, is sized."},
 {"u":"30c3.1","q":"What is the total ∫ f dm of a sized f ≥ 0, and what does the total-from-below technique say?","a":"the least upper bound of the totals of the step functions below f; and every sized f ≥ 0 is the limit of a rising list of step functions below it","w":["the greatest lower bound of the totals of step functions above f; f is the limit of a falling list","the area of rung fifteen; f is a step function","the total of f over its largest interval; f is the limit of its values"],"why":"A total cannot see what happens on a set of size 0."},
 {"u":"30c4.1","q":"What does the agrees-with-area technique say, and what does the new total measure that the old area did not?","a":"for f ≥ 0 continuous on [u, v], its total is rung fifteen’s area ∫ f(x) dx; the new total also measures functions the old could not, as 10d’s q, which has no area there and total 0 here","w":["the new total is twice the old area; it measures nothing new","the new total differs from the area for continuous f; it measures only step functions","the new total agrees with the area for every function; q has area 1"],"why":"Nothing rung fifteen built is lost."},
 {"u":"30c7.1","q":"What is wrong with “∫ f dm = 0, so f = 0 at every point”?","a":"for f ≥ 0 a total of 0 says only that f is 0 except on a set of size 0: q is 1 at every fraction and has total 0","w":["a total of 0 means f is negative somewhere","a total of 0 means f has no total","nothing; a total sees every point"],"why":"A claim about the whole, not about each point."},
 {"u":"30c5.1","q":"Which bounded functions on [u, v] are integrable by lower and upper sums? Why is 1/q at each fraction p/q, 0 elsewhere, integrable, and 10d’s q not?","a":"those whose breaks form a set of outer size 0; the first breaks only at the fractions, a countable set of size 0, and the second breaks at every point of [0, 1]","w":["those with finitely many breaks; the first has none, and the second has endlessly many","those that are continuous; neither is, and both have the total 0","those whose breaks are all jumps; the first has jumps, and the second has endless breaks"],"why":"30c5: where the swing is at least t the gap M − m is at least t, so a small U − L forces those points into short pieces; and off a cheap cover of them the swing is small."},
 {"u":"30c6.1","q":"Where is the slope of an area function equal to the function? What can go wrong at a break?","a":"at every point outside a set of size 0: at least wherever f is continuous; at a break F may have no slope, as at the corner under a jump, or a slope that is not f there, as under a single raised point","w":["at every point, since F is always smooth; nothing can go wrong","only where f is 0; at a break F jumps","at the fractions only; at a break F is not defined"],"why":"30c6: F′(c) = f(c) wherever f is continuous (16b), and the breaks of an integrable f have outer size 0 (30c5)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30d1.1","q":"What does the rising-limit technique say, and what does it give about sums and multiples of totals?","a":"if 0 ≤ f₁ ≤ f₂ ≤ ⋯ are sized and settle at f at every x, then ∫fₙ settles at ∫f; with rising steps it gives ∫(f + g) = ∫f + ∫g and ∫cf = c∫f for sized f, g ≥ 0 and c ≥ 0","w":["for any list settling at f the totals settle at ∫f; totals multiply","for a rising list the totals settle at something no smaller than ∫f; totals add only for step functions","for a falling list the totals settle at ∫f; ∫cf = ∫f"],"why":"Rising lists cannot lose total."},
 {"u":"30d2.1","q":"What is the lower limit of a list of reals, what does the lower-limit technique say, and how can its two sides differ?","a":"the number the greatest lower bounds of the tails rise to; for sized fₙ ≥ 0 the total of the lower limit is at most the lower limit of the totals; a bump of total 1 sliding off to the right has lower limit 0 at every point, total 0, while every total is 1","w":["the least member of the list; the total of the lower limit equals the lower limit of the totals","the limit of the list; the total of the lower limit is at least the lower limit of the totals","the lower limit is 0 for every list; the two sides never differ"],"why":"The bump took its total with it."},
 {"u":"30d3.1","q":"What does the fenced-limit technique say, what does the fence ask of the list that 23e did not, and what does it not ask?","a":"if fₙ settles at f at every x and |fₙ| ≤ g for every n with ∫g finite, then ∫fₙ settles at ∫f; it asks for one fence with a finite total over the whole list, and it does not ask the list to settle everywhere at once, only point by point","w":["if fₙ settles at f then ∫fₙ settles at ∫f, with no fence needed","the fence asks that fₙ settle everywhere at once and that g be bounded","the fence may have an endless total so long as each fₙ has a finite one"],"why":"Each point at its own pace; only the room to escape is gone."},
 {"u":"30d4.1","q":"What is wrong with “fₙ → f at every point, so ∫fₙ → ∫f”?","a":"settling point by point lets total escape: the sliding bump settles at 0 everywhere and keeps total 1; a fence with a finite total is what stops it","w":["it fails only when f is not sized","the totals always settle, but at 2∫f","nothing; limits pass through totals"],"why":"g = 1 fences the bump and has an endless total: not fence enough."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30e1.1","q":"What is the outer size of a set in the plane, when is such a set sized, and what is the slice Eₓ?","a":"the greatest lower bound of the total areas of countably many rectangles that cover it; it is sized when it passes the split test with that outer size; the slice Eₓ is the set of y with (x, y) in E","w":["the area of the smallest rectangle round it; it is sized when it is a rectangle; the slice is its shadow on the x-axis","the product of the outer sizes of its two shadows; every set is sized; the slice is the set of x with (x, y) in E","the length of its edge; it is sized when its edge is; the slice is its edge at x"],"why":"The same construction as on the line, with rectangles for intervals."},
 {"u":"30e2.1","q":"What does the slices technique say?","a":"for a sized set E in the plane, except on a size-0 set of x the slice Eₓ is sized, x ↦ m(Eₓ) is sized, and m₂(E) = ∫ m(Eₓ) dx","w":["every slice of a sized set is sized, and m₂(E) is the largest m(Eₓ)","the slices of a sized set may all fail to be sized, so m₂(E) cannot be read from them","m₂(E) = m(Eₓ) for any one x"],"why":"16j’s volume by cross-sections, for every sized set."},
 {"u":"30e3.1","q":"What does the either-order-sized technique say, and when may a function that takes both signs be totalled in either order?","a":"for sized f ≥ 0 on the plane the plane total equals the total along y then x, and along x then y; a function of both signs may be totalled in either order whenever the total of |f| is finite, which can itself be checked in either order","w":["any sized f can be totalled in either order; signs do not matter","only continuous f on a rectangle can be totalled in either order","a function of both signs can never be totalled in either order"],"why":"28h’s either-order, for every sized function that allows it."},
 {"u":"30e4.1","q":"Why do the two orders disagree for the staircase of squares, and why does that not break the either-order technique?","a":"along one order every column’s +1 and −1 squares cancel except the first, giving 1, and along the other every row cancels, giving 0; the total of |f| is endless, endlessly many unit squares, so the technique does not apply","w":["the staircase is not sized, so neither order has a total","the two orders agree once the squares are smoothed","the technique is broken, and holds only for f ≥ 0 on rectangles"],"why":"Check |f| first."},
 {"u":"30e5.1","q":"What is wrong with “any f can be totalled in either order”?","a":"the order may be swapped for f ≥ 0, or when the total of |f| is finite; the staircase gives a finite answer each way and they differ","w":["the order may be swapped only for step functions","the order may never be swapped","nothing; 28h proved it for every function"],"why":"Finite each way is not enough."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30f1.1","q":"What are the three things in a chance space, and what does the chance technique say?","a":"a set Ω of outcomes, its events, and a chance P on them that is a size with P(Ω) = 1; then P(not A) = 1 − P(A), and P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B)","w":["a coin, a die and a deck; P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) always","outcomes, events and a chance with P(Ω) = 0; P(not A) = P(A)","outcomes, averages and spreads; P(A or B) = P(A) P(B)"],"why":"Not, or and and on events are rung 1’s on claims about the outcome."},
 {"u":"30f2.1","q":"What is the chance of A given B, and what does it mean for A and B to be independent?","a":"P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) for P(B) > 0; A and B are independent when P(A ∩ B) = P(A) P(B), which for P(B) > 0 says P(A | B) = P(A): knowing B tells nothing about A","w":["P(A | B) = P(A) + P(B); independent means they cannot both happen","P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) P(B); independent means P(A) = P(B)","P(A | B) = P(B | A); independent means P(A ∩ B) = 0"],"why":"If A and B are independent, so are A and not B."},
 {"u":"30f3.1","q":"What do the whole-chance and turn-around techniques say, and what kind of claim is a chance?","a":"for separate events Bₖ of positive chance that fill Ω, P(A) = Σ P(A | Bₖ) P(Bₖ); and P(B | A) = P(A | B) P(B)/P(A); a chance is a law stated with its exception rate, exact about the many and silent about the one","w":["P(A) = Σ P(A | Bₖ); P(B | A) = P(A | B); a chance is a claim about the next case","P(A) = Σ P(Bₖ); P(B | A) = P(A) P(B); a chance is a guess with no check","P(A) = P(A | B) for any B; P(B | A) = 1 − P(A | B); a chance is true of each single case"],"why":"A 30 percent forecast is checked over many forecasts, not one."},
 {"u":"30f4.1","q":"What is wrong with “P(A | B) = P(B | A)”?","a":"the two given chances are turned round by P(B)/P(A) and can be far apart: a test that flags 99 percent of the ill can still be wrong for most of the people it flags","w":["they differ only when A and B are independent","P(A | B) = 1 − P(B | A)","nothing; given runs both ways"],"why":"The turn-around technique needs the chances before."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30g1.1","q":"What is a chance variable, what is its average, and what does the average-adds technique say?","a":"a sized function X on the outcomes; its average E X is its total against the chance; E(aX + bY) = a E X + b E Y, and X ≤ Y gives E X ≤ E Y","w":["an outcome; its average is its most likely value; averages add only for independent variables","a chance between 0 and 1; its average is ½; E(X + Y) = E X · E Y","a sized function; its average is its middle value; E(aX) = E X"],"why":"Two dice average 7, with no need to list 36 outcomes."},
 {"u":"30g2.1","q":"What are the spread, the spread width and the co-spread, and what does the spread-of-a-sum technique say?","a":"the spread Var X is the average of (X − μ)², the width σ its square root, and the co-spread Cov(X, Y) the average of (X − μ)(Y − ν); Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y + 2 Cov(X, Y)","w":["the spread is the largest value less the smallest; Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y always","the spread is E|X − μ|; the width is its square; Var(X + Y) = Var X · Var Y","the spread is E X² ; the co-spread is E X · E Y; Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y − Cov(X, Y)"],"why":"One fair die: Var X = 35/12, σ ≈ 1.71."},
 {"u":"30g3.1","q":"When are two chance variables independent, and what do the independent-averages-multiply and independent-spreads-add techniques say?","a":"when every event about X is independent of every event about Y; then E(XY) = E X · E Y, so Cov(X, Y) = 0 and Var(X + Y) = Var X + Var Y: the widths combine like the two short sides of a right triangle","w":["when Cov(X, Y) = 0; then E(X + Y) = E X + E Y, which fails otherwise","when X and Y never take the same value; then their widths add","when they have the same chance pattern; then Var(X + Y) = 4 Var X"],"why":"Widths 3 g and 4 g make 5 g, not 7 g."},
 {"u":"30g4.1","q":"What is wrong with “averages add only for independent parts”?","a":"averages always add, E(X + Y) = E X + E Y, whatever ties X to Y; it is spreads that need independence to add","w":["averages never add","averages add only when the spreads are equal","nothing; independence is needed for both"],"why":"The total adds and scales, with no condition."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30h1.1","q":"What do the stray-bound and spread-bound techniques say, and what does the spread-bound need to know about X?","a":"for X ≥ 0 and a > 0, P(X ≥ a) ≤ E X/a; and P(|X − μ| ≥ kσ) ≤ 1/k² for every k > 0; the spread-bound needs nothing about the shape of X but its average and spread","w":["P(X ≥ a) ≥ E X/a; P(|X − μ| ≥ kσ) = 1/k²; X must be bell-shaped","P(X ≥ a) ≤ a/E X; P(|X − μ| ≥ kσ) ≤ 1/k; X must be bounded","both hold only for whole-number X; the spread-bound needs the chance function"],"why":"At most 1 in 4 lies two widths out, whatever X is."},
 {"u":"30h2.1","q":"What does the averages-settle technique say, and how is an average from tries reported?","a":"for every ε > 0, P(|X̄ₙ − μ| ≥ ε) ≤ σ²/(nε²), which heads to 0 as n grows; an average from tries is reported with its width, σ over the root of the number of tries","w":["X̄ₙ equals μ once n is large; it is reported bare","P(|X̄ₙ − μ| ≥ ε) ≤ σ/n; it is reported with the width σ/n","the chance of a stray stays fixed as n grows; it is reported with σ"],"why":"Quadruple the poll, and the width only halves."},
 {"u":"30h3.1","q":"What does the finitely-often technique say, and what does it not need?","a":"if Σ P(Aₙ) is finite, then with chance 1 only finitely many of the events happen; it needs nothing about whether the events are independent","w":["if Σ P(Aₙ) is finite, none of the events happens; it needs independence","if Σ P(Aₙ) is endless, infinitely many happen; it needs nothing","if each P(Aₙ) is below 1, finitely many happen; it needs equal chances"],"why":"It comes from counting alone."},
 {"u":"30h4.1","q":"What does the surely-settle technique say, and what more does it say than averages-settle?","a":"for independent X₁, X₂, … with the same chance pattern, average μ and E X⁴ finite, with chance 1 the running average X̄ₙ settles at μ; averages-settle says a stray is unlikely at each large n, this says that along the whole run the strays stop","w":["X̄ₙ settles at μ in every run without exception; it says the same as averages-settle","X̄ₙ is within ε of μ with chance heading to 1; it says less than averages-settle","X̄ₙ settles at some number that depends on the run; it says nothing about μ"],"why":"The demand on E X⁴ only makes the proof short."},
 {"u":"30h5.1","q":"In what way is “with chance 1” weaker than 3b2’s □, and what does “very likely” allow that “surely” does not?","a":"□ counts no exceptions, while chance 1 allows them on a set of chance 0; “very likely” at each large n still allows a stray now and then for ever, while “surely” says that with chance 1 the strays stop","w":["chance 1 is the same as □; very likely and surely are the same","chance 1 is stronger than □; very likely forbids every stray","chance 1 allows exceptions half the time; surely allows endlessly many strays"],"why":"Two logics of necessity: one counts no exceptions, one counts them and reports the count."},
 {"u":"30h6.1","q":"What is wrong with “after five tails a fair coin is due a head”?","a":"the tosses are independent: the next toss has chance ½ whatever came before; the average settles by swamping, not by paying back","w":["after five tails a tail is more likely","the coin is due a head only after ten tails","nothing; averages-settle demands it"],"why":"The coin has no memory."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30i1.1","q":"What is the chance function of a whole-number chance variable X, and what does the chances-from-G technique say?","a":"G(s) = E s^X = Σ P(X = k) sᵏ; then P(X = k) = G⁽ᵏ⁾(0)/k!, so two whole-number chance variables with the same chance function have the same chances","w":["G(s) = P(X ≤ s); P(X = k) = G(k)","G(s) = E e^(sX); P(X = k) = G(k)/k!","G(s) = Σ k sᵏ; the chance function does not fix the chances"],"why":"A rare count of average λ has G(s) = e^(λ(s − 1))."},
 {"u":"30i2.1","q":"What does the average-from-G technique say, and why is G′(1) read as a limit as s rises to 1?","a":"E X = G′(1), meaning the number G′(s) heads to as s rises to 1; s = 1 is the end of the power sum’s sure reach, where the slope may not exist or may be endless, and the limit is right in either case","w":["E X = G(1); it is a limit because G(1) may be above 1","E X = G′(0); the limit is needed because G jumps at 0","E X = G′(1), read directly, since every power sum has a slope at 1"],"why":"Likewise G″(1) = E X(X − 1)."},
 {"u":"30i3.1","q":"What does the sums-multiply technique say, and which technique of 30g carries its proof?","a":"for independent whole-number X and Y, the chance function of X + Y is G_X(s) G_Y(s); the proof is E(s^X s^Y) = E s^X · E s^Y, the independent-averages-multiply technique","w":["the chance function of X + Y is G_X + G_Y; by average-adds","the chance function of X + Y is G_X(G_Y(s)); by the chain technique","it holds for any X and Y; by spread-of-a-sum"],"why":"A chance pattern is known by what its chance function does."},
 {"u":"30i4.1","q":"What does the dying-out technique say, and how does the average number of children decide whether the line can survive?","a":"the line dies out with chance q, the least s in [0, 1] with G(s) = s; with average at most 1 it dies out with chance 1, unless every member has exactly one child, and with average above 1 it survives with chance 1 − q > 0","w":["the line dies out with chance G(0); an average of exactly 1 keeps it going for ever","the line dies out with chance ½ whatever the average","the line survives with chance q; an average below 1 makes survival certain"],"why":"qₙ = G(qₙ₋₁), climbing to the first crossing."},
 {"u":"30i5.1","q":"Why is “the chance function of X + Y is the product of those of X and Y” not a technique for any X and Y, and why does G(1) not give the average?","a":"the product needs independence: for Y = X the sum 2X has G(s²), not G(s)²; and G(1) is the sum of all the chances, 1 always, while the average is the slope G′(1)","w":["the product holds for any X and Y with the same chance pattern; G(1) is the average only for dice","the product never holds; G(1) is the spread","the product needs X and Y to be separate events; G(1) = 0"],"why":"Value at 1, slope at 1: different readings."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30j1.1","q":"What does the bell-total technique say, what is the trick of its proof, and what is the bell Z, with its density φ?","a":"∫ e^(−x²) dx over the whole line is √π; the trick is to square it into a total over the plane, where round measures bring an r that makes the slope undoable; Z is the chance variable with density φ(x) = e^(−x²/2)/√(2π), average 0 and spread 1","w":["the total is π; the trick is an undoing of e^(−x²); φ(x) = e^(−x²)","the total is √π; the trick is the power sum; Z has average 1 and spread 0","the total is 1; no trick is needed; φ(x) = e^(−|x|)/2"],"why":"No formula undoes e^(−x²); the plane gives only the whole-line total."},
 {"u":"30j2.1","q":"What is the average-function of X, and what does the average-function technique say?","a":"M(t) = E e^(tX); if it is finite on an interval around 0, then near 0, M(t) = 1 + t E X + t² E X²/2 + r(t) with r(t)/t² heading to 0, and for independent X and Y the average-function of X + Y is M_X M_Y","w":["M(t) = E X^t; near 0 it is 1 + t; for independent X and Y the functions add","M(t) = E s^X; it gives the chances by slopes at 0","M(t) = e^(t E X); it holds no more than the average"],"why":"The bell’s is e^(t²/2)."},
 {"u":"30j3.1","q":"What does the central limit technique say, and what alone of the parts survives in the limit?","a":"for independent parts with one chance pattern, the sum centred at its average and scaled by its width has an average-function heading to e^(t²/2), so its chances head to the bell’s areas; of the parts only the spread survives","w":["the sum itself heads to the bell; the average of the parts survives","the scaled sum heads to the chance pattern of one part; every detail of the parts survives","the scaled sum heads to a flat pattern; nothing survives"],"why":"The bell is the shape a sum passes through when its parts have faded into their spread."},
 {"u":"30j4.1","q":"Why does the sum X₁ + ⋯ + Xₙ itself not head to the bell, and why are independent parts needed?","a":"its average nμ and its width σ√n both run off as n grows, so it must be centred and scaled first; and with X₁ = X₂ = ⋯ the scaled sum is √n times one part, which never nears the bell","w":["the sum does head to the bell without scaling; independence only speeds it","the sum heads to 0; parts that are equal head to the bell fastest","the sum has no average; independence is needed only for whole-number parts"],"why":"Fading needs many parts that do not move together."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30k1.1","q":"What is a fair game, and what does the fair-stays-fair technique say?","a":"a list of fortunes X₀, X₁, … in which, given any history up to round n, the average of Xₙ₊₁ is Xₙ; then E Xₙ = E X₀ for every n","w":["a game with equal chances of winning each round; then Xₙ = X₀","a game whose rounds are independent; then E Xₙ grows like √n","a game in which the average of Xₙ₊₁ is 0; then E Xₙ = 0 after one round"],"why":"Whole-chance over the histories, and induction on n."},
 {"u":"30k2.1","q":"What is a stopping technique, when is it bounded, and what does the stopping-keeps technique say?","a":"a technique T for when to stop that uses only the history so far; it is bounded when some fixed round is never passed; for a bounded stopping technique in a fair game, E X_T = E X₀","w":["a technique that may look one round ahead; bounded when the stakes are; E X_T ≥ E X₀","any time to stop; bounded when the game is; E X_T = E X₀ for every T","a technique that uses only the history; bounded when it stops with chance 1; E X_T = X₀ + 1"],"why":"Whether T > n is settled by the past."},
 {"u":"30k3.1","q":"What is a system, what does the no-system technique say, and why does doubling after each loss not break it?","a":"a technique that sets each stake from the history so far; in a fair game every system with a bounded stopping technique has average winnings 0; doubling needs stakes with no ceiling and a stopping time with no bound, both outside the technique","w":["a technique that picks the winning rounds; some systems win on average; doubling is one","a technique that sets stakes from the past; every system loses on average; doubling loses fastest","a technique that stops after a win; the average winnings are the first stake; doubling proves it"],"why":"With a ceiling, a long run of losses brings the average back to 0."},
 {"u":"30k4.1","q":"What does the ruin-chance technique say, and why does its proof need the fenced-limit technique?","a":"a fair coin walk started at k reaches N before 0 with chance k/N; the stopping time is not bounded, so stopping-keeps is used on the walk stopped at round n, whose fortunes stay between 0 and N, and the limit is passed through under that fence","w":["the chance is ½ from every start; the proof needs no limit","the chance is k/N; the fenced limit is needed because the walk may grow without bound","the chance is (N − k)/N; the proof uses surely-settle"],"why":"From 90 aiming at 100: nine wins in ten, and on average nothing."},
 {"u":"30k5.1","q":"Why is stopping-keeps not a technique for any stopping technique, and why is “stop the round before a loss” not a stopping technique?","a":"stop the first time you are 1 ahead: X_T = 1 always, yet X₀ = 0, and that technique has no bound; and stopping before a loss needs the next round’s result, which the history so far does not hold","w":["stopping-keeps holds for every technique that stops with chance 1; stopping before a loss is a stopping technique with a bound","stopping-keeps fails for bounded techniques; stopping before a loss is allowed in fair games","both are fine once stakes are capped"],"why":"No looking ahead, and a bound."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30l1.1","q":"What does the equal-cases technique say, and what in it is a guess about the world rather than a fact of counting?","a":"if Ω has n outcomes each with the same chance, each has chance 1/n and an event of k outcomes has chance k/n; that the outcomes are equally likely is the guess, to be checked against tallies","w":["any n outcomes have chance 1/n each; nothing in it is a guess","an event of k outcomes has chance k; the count n is the guess","the chance of an event is k/n only for dice; the guess is that the die is thrown"],"why":"A die with a chipped corner does not make the guess good."},
 {"u":"30l2.1","q":"What does the branch-product technique say, what does the at-least-one technique say, and what does it need of the events?","a":"the chance of a path through a tree is the product of the given chances on its branches, and an event’s chance is the sum over its separate paths; the chance of at least one of A₁, …, Aₙ is 1 − (1 − P(A₁))⋯(1 − P(Aₙ)), which needs the events independent","w":["the chance of a path is the sum of its branches; at least one has chance P(A₁) + ⋯ + P(Aₙ)","the chance of a path is its last branch; at least one has chance 1 − P(A₁)⋯P(Aₙ) for any events","the chance of a path is the product; at least one needs the events separate"],"why":"Chances of at least one do not grow in proportion to the throws."},
 {"u":"30l3.1","q":"What are the odds of A, what does the odds-update technique say, and what does the ratio alone not tell you?","a":"the odds are P(A)/P(not A); the odds of A given E are the odds of A times the ratio P(E | A)/P(E | not A); the ratio alone does not give the odds after: those also need the odds before, which come from outside the test","w":["the odds are P(A); the odds given E are the ratio itself","the odds are 1 − P(A); the odds given E are the odds plus the ratio","the odds are P(A)/P(not A); the ratio alone gives the chance that A is true"],"why":"A ratio of 10 on a rare condition still leaves about one in eleven."},
 {"u":"30l4.1","q":"Why is P(E | A) = 0.9 not the chance that a flagged person has the condition, and why is the chance of at least one six in 4 throws not 4 × 1/6?","a":"0.9 is the chance of a flag given the condition; the chance of the condition given a flag also needs how rare the condition is; and the throws can give more than one six, so the chance is 1 − (5/6)⁴ ≈ 0.518, not 0.667","w":["0.9 is the chance of the condition given a flag; four throws give 4/6","the chance of the condition is 0.1; four throws give (1/6)⁴","both are right for independent events"],"why":"Given runs one way; at least one runs through none."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30m"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30m"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30m1.1","q":"What does it mean for X to have density f, and what does the density-average technique say?","a":"the chance that X lands in a stretch is the area under f over that stretch, P(a < X ≤ b) = ∫ f from a to b; the average of g(X) is ∫ g(x) f(x) dx","w":["the chance that X equals x is f(x); the average of g(X) is g of the average of X","the chance that X lands in a stretch is f at its midpoint; the average of g(X) is ∫ g(x) dx","the chance that X is below x is f(x); the average of g(X) is ∫ f(g(x)) dx"],"why":"The density is weighed by area, never read by height."},
 {"u":"30m2.1","q":"What is the exponential density of rate λ, and what does the forgets-the-wait technique say?","a":"λe^(−λx) for x ≥ 0, with average 1/λ and spread width 1/λ; having already waited s changes nothing, P(X > s + t given X > s) = P(X > t)","w":["λe^(−λx), with average λ and width λ; having waited s, the rest of the wait is shorter","e^(−λx)/λ, with average 1/λ and width 1/λ²; having waited s, the rest of the wait is longer","λe^(−λx), with average 1/λ and width 1/λ; having waited s, the chance of waiting t more is P(X > t) − P(X > s)"],"why":"e^(−λ(s+t)) / e^(−λs) = e^(−λt)."},
 {"u":"30m3.1","q":"What does the stretched-density technique say, and what does the bell-series technique give?","a":"Y = μ + σX has density (1/σ) f((y − μ)/σ); the bell’s area from 0 to x is an alternating sum of shrinking terms, so the error of a partial sum is below the first term left out","w":["Y = μ + σX has density σ f((y − μ)/σ); the bell’s area is a closed formula with no error","Y = μ + σX has density f(y − μ); the bell’s area is a sum whose error grows with every term","Y = μ + σX has density (1/σ) f(σy + μ); the bell’s area is a sum of positive terms bounded by its last term"],"why":"P(0 ≤ Z ≤ 1) = (1/√(2π))(1 − 1/6 + 1/40 − 1/336 + …) ≈ 0.3413."},
 {"u":"30m4.1","q":"Why is a height of a density not a chance, and why is the density of Y = 2X not 2f(2y)?","a":"only areas under a density are chances, and a height may be above 1; stretching X to twice the width halves the height, so the density of Y is (1/2) f(y/2) and the area stays 1","w":["a height is a chance only when it is below 1; the density of Y = 2X is 2f(y/2)","a height is the chance of that exact value; the density of Y = 2X is f(y/2)","a height is a chance per trial; the density of Y = 2X is f(2y), since only the scale changes"],"why":"2f(2y) squeezes the density to half the width; Y = 2X is wider, not narrower."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30n"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30n"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30n1.1","q":"What do the root-n technique and the back-at-start technique say?","a":"after n independent steps of spread width s the place has spread width s√n; the fair coin walk is back at 0 after 2n steps with chance C(2n, n)/4ⁿ, about 1/√(πn)","w":["after n steps the place has spread width sn; the walk is back at 0 after 2n steps with chance 1/2","after n steps the place has spread width s√n; the walk is back at 0 after 2n steps with chance 1/2ⁿ","after n steps the place has spread width s/√n; the walk is back at 0 after 2n steps with chance about 1/(πn)"],"why":"Spreads add, so widths add as squares."},
 {"u":"30n2.1","q":"What does the spread-in-time technique say, and what does a measured spread fix about the walk?","a":"steps of length h every τ give spread 2Dt along a line with D = h²/(2τ), and 6Dt in space; a measured spread fixes D alone, and leaves open h and τ separately","w":["the spread is 2Dt with D = h/τ; a measured spread fixes h and τ both","the spread is Dt² with D = h²/(2τ); a measured spread fixes h, and leaves τ open","the spread is 2Dt with D = h²/(2τ); a measured spread fixes the step length h, since τ is always known"],"why":"Halve h and quarter τ, and the spread is the same."},
 {"u":"30n3.1","q":"What do the exit-time technique and the comes-back technique say?","a":"the fair coin walk started at k between 0 and N reaches 0 or N in a finite time with chance 1, of average k(N − k) steps; the walk on the whole line comes back to its start with chance 1, though the average time to come back is endless","w":["the walk leaves in an average of N steps; on the whole line it comes back with chance 1/2","the walk leaves in an average of k(N − k) steps; on the whole line it comes back with chance 1 in an average of 2 steps","the walk may never leave; on the whole line it never comes back once it is far away"],"why":"Sₙ² − n is a fair game, stopped at the exit."},
 {"u":"30n4.1","q":"Why is the spread width after n steps not ns, and why does a walk at +20 not lean back toward 0?","a":"the steps go both ways and partly cancel, so their spreads add and the width is s√n; each next step is independent of the place reached, so from +20 the average place stays +20","w":["the width is ns only for large n; a walk at +20 leans back because heads and tails must even out","the steps shrink as the walk goes on; a walk at +20 leans back at half the rate it went out","the width is s√n because half the steps are lost; a walk at +20 leans further out, since it is on a run"],"why":"The coin has no memory of where the walk stands."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30o"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30o"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30o1.1","q":"What is a rare-arrival stream of rate λ, and what does the first-gap technique say?","a":"a placing of arrivals in time in which the count in any stretch of length t is a rare count of average λt, with counts in stretches that do not overlap independent; the wait for the next arrival is exponential of rate λ, whatever happened before","w":["arrivals spaced exactly 1/λ apart; the wait for the next is at most 1/λ","a stream whose count in a stretch of length t is exactly λt; the wait is 1/λ every time","a stream of independent counts of average λt; the wait for the next arrival shortens the longer one has waited"],"why":"P(no arrival in t) = e^(−λt)."},
 {"u":"30o2.1","q":"What does the first-of-two technique say, and what do two independent rare-arrival streams make together?","a":"of two independent exponential waits of rates λ and μ, the earlier is exponential of rate λ + μ, and the first one wins with chance λ/(λ + μ); the two streams together are one rare-arrival stream of rate λ + μ","w":["the earlier wait has rate λμ, and the first wins with chance 1/2; together the streams have rate λμ","the earlier wait has the larger of the two rates; together the streams have the larger rate","the earlier wait has rate λ + μ, and the first wins with chance μ/(λ + μ); together the streams are not a rare-arrival stream"],"why":"P(both waits exceed t) = e^(−λt) e^(−μt)."},
 {"u":"30o3.1","q":"What is a chain that forgets its past, why is the working-or-broken machine one, and what does the two-state technique say?","a":"a chain whose chances for what comes next depend only on the state it is in now; the machine’s waits to break and to be mended are exponential and so forget how long they have run; its chance of working goes to β/(α + β), with the starting difference fading as e^(−(α+β)t)","w":["a chain that returns to its start; the machine is one because it is always mended; its chance of working stays where it began","a chain with independent states; the machine is one because breaking and mending are independent; its chance of working goes to 1/2","a chain whose next chances depend on its whole past; the machine is one because it wears; its chance of working falls to 0"],"why":"p(t) = β/(α + β) + (p(0) − β/(α + β)) e^(−(α+β)t), with α the breaking rate and β the mending rate."},
 {"u":"30o4.1","q":"What are the steady chances of the line at one window, and what does the steady-line technique say?","a":"with ρ = λ/μ below 1, the chance of n in the system is (1 − ρ)ρⁿ; the window is busy a share ρ of the time and the average number in the system is ρ/(1 − ρ)","w":["with ρ = λ/μ below 1, the chance of n is ρⁿ; the average number in the system is ρ","with ρ = μ/λ, the chance of n is (1 − ρ)ρⁿ; the average number is 1/ρ","for any ρ, the chance of n is (1 − ρ)ρⁿ; the average number is ρ/(1 + ρ)"],"why":"At ρ = 0.9 the average is 9; at ρ = 0.99 it is 99."},
 {"u":"30o5.1","q":"Why does a rider who arrives at a random moment wait the whole average gap, and why do arrivals at 50 an hour and service at 50 an hour not keep the line short?","a":"the stream forgets, so the wait from any moment is exponential with the full average, and long gaps cover more moments than short ones; at ρ = 1 the steady chances (1 − ρ)ρⁿ are all 0, so there is no steady line and it grows without bound","w":["the rider waits half the gap on average; at ρ = 1 the line holds steady at one customer","the rider waits the whole gap because buses bunch; at ρ = 1 the line empties as often as it fills, so it stays short","the rider waits twice the gap; at ρ = 1 the average line is 1/(1 − ρ) = 1"],"why":"ρ/(1 − ρ) has no value at ρ = 1."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30p"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30p"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30p1.1","q":"What is the chance grid of a chain, and what does the chance-grid technique say?","a":"the grid P whose column for a state lists the chances of each state one step later, so every column adds to 1; the list of chances after n steps is xₙ = Pⁿx₀","w":["the grid whose rows add to 0; the list after n steps is nPx₀","the grid of chances of ever reaching each state; the list after n steps is P x₀ⁿ","the grid whose columns add to 1; the list after n steps is x₀ + nP"],"why":"Two chance grids run in a row give a chance grid."},
 {"u":"30p2.1","q":"What is a settled list, and what does the one-rank technique say?","a":"a list of chances π with Pπ = π; if every entry of P is above 0, with least entry m, there is exactly one settled list and every start heads to it, by the shrinking-differences technique: P shrinks any difference of two chance lists by the factor 1 − m at least","w":["a list with Pπ = 0; if every entry of P is above 0, every list is settled","a list of equal chances; if every entry of P is above 0, the chain heads to equal chances","a list with Pπ = π; if every entry of P is above 0, there is one settled list, but which start reaches it depends on the start"],"why":"|Pⁿx₀ − π| ≤ 2(1 − m)ⁿ."},
 {"u":"30p3.1","q":"What does the fading-start technique say for two states, and what does the share-of-time technique say?","a":"with crossing chances α and β, π = (β, α)/(α + β) and xₙ = π + (1 − α − β)ⁿ(x₀ − π); over a long run the share of steps spent in each state heads to its entry in π","w":["π = (α, β)/(α + β) and the start never fades; the share of time in each state is 1/2","π = (β, α)/(α + β) and xₙ = π + n(1 − α − β)(x₀ − π); the share of time depends on the start","π = (1/2, 1/2) and xₙ = π for every n; the share of time in each state heads to its entry in x₀"],"why":"The start fades by the factor 1 − α − β each step."},
 {"u":"30p4.1","q":"Why is the chance of wet in two days not 0.7², and why is (4, 3) not the settled list of the wet-and-dry grid?","a":"0.7² counts only the road through a wet day, and the road through a dry day must be added; a settled list is a list of chances, so (4, 3) must be divided by 7 to add to 1","w":["0.7² is right for two days, and wrong only from three days on; (4, 3) is settled, since P(4, 3) = (4, 3)","0.7² leaves out that the days are independent; (4, 3) fails because its entries are not equal","the chance is 2 × 0.7; (4, 3) fails because P(4, 3) is not (4, 3)"],"why":"(4, 3) is kept by P but is not a list of chances; (4/7, 3/7) is."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30q"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30q"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30q1.1","q":"What does the sample-average technique say about X̄, and why do n independent doubts of width σ add to width σ√n, not nσ?","a":"E X̄ = μ and Var X̄ = σ²/n, so the width of X̄ is σ/√n; independent misses partly cancel, so their spreads add, nσ², and the width is the root of that","w":["E X̄ = μ and Var X̄ = σ², whatever n is; the doubts add to nσ","E X̄ = μ/n and Var X̄ = σ²/n; the doubts add to σ√n because half of them are lost","E X̄ = μ and Var X̄ = σ²/n²; the doubts add to σ, since they all cancel"],"why":"To halve the doubt, take four times the sample."},
 {"u":"30q2.1","q":"Why does the sample-spread technique divide by n − 1 rather than n?","a":"the misses are taken from X̄, which sits closer to the readings than μ does, so their squares come out too small on average; dividing by n − 1 makes E S² = σ²","w":["one reading is thrown away as a check on the rest","dividing by n − 1 makes S² smaller, as a caution","n − 1 is used only for small samples; for large ones dividing by n gives E S² = σ² exactly"],"why":"The squares of the misses from X̄ average (n − 1)σ², not nσ²."},
 {"u":"30q3.1","q":"What does the bell-cover technique say, and to what does the 95% belong?","a":"the interval X̄ ± 1.96σ/√n covers μ with a chance heading to 0.95 as n grows; the 95% belongs to the method, which covers in 95 of 100 uses, not to one interval once built","w":["the interval X̄ ± 1.96σ covers μ with chance 0.95; the 95% belongs to the interval once built","the interval X̄ ± 1.96σ/√n holds 95% of the readings; the 95% belongs to the sample","the interval X̄ ± 1.96σ/√n covers X̄ with chance 0.95; the 95% belongs to μ"],"why":"Once the numbers are in, the interval either holds μ or does not."},
 {"u":"30q4.1","q":"Why does a small sample with σ unknown need a wider table than the bell, and why is its 0.95 exact?","a":"S stands in for σ and wobbles itself, which fattens the tails, so the table number is larger, 2.365 for 7 degrees against 1.96; for bell readings the way T = (X̄ − μ)/(S/√n) is spread depends only on n − 1 and is known exactly, with no limit taken","w":["small samples have larger σ; the 0.95 is exact because S = σ for bell readings","the bell table is wrong below n = 30; the 0.95 is exact because the table was measured","S is always below σ, so the table shrinks to make up; the 0.95 is a limit as n grows"],"why":"As the degrees grow, the table number comes down to 1.96."},
 {"u":"30q5.1","q":"Why is “the chance that μ lies between 5.06 and 6.19 is 0.95” a move to watch out for?","a":"μ is a fixed number and the two ends are now fixed numbers, so nothing in the claim is left to chance; the 0.95 is the chance, before sampling, that the method’s interval will cover μ","w":["the chance is 0.95 only if σ is known","the chance is really 0.05, since 0.95 is the chance of missing","nothing is wrong with it; it is what the interval says"],"why":"The interval is random; μ is not."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30r"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30r"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30r1.1","q":"What is the likelihood of a sample, and why may the tops of L be found from ℓ?","a":"L(θ) is the chance, or density, of the sample that was seen, read as a function of θ; it is not a chance of θ, and it need not add to 1 over θ; ln rises wherever its input rises, so ℓ = ln L tops where L tops","w":["L(θ) is the chance that θ is the true value, given the sample; ℓ tops where L is least","L(θ) is the chance of a sample not yet seen; ℓ has the same value as L at the top","L(θ) is the density of θ; ℓ and L top at different places, which ℓ corrects"],"why":"A product of n chances becomes a sum of n logs."},
 {"u":"30r2.1","q":"What best guesses do the rare-guess and gap-guess techniques give, and where does the best guess sit when every count is 0?","a":"for rare counts λ̂ is the average count k̄, and for exponential gaps λ̂ = 1/x̄; when every count is 0 the top sits at the end λ̂ = 0, where the slope of ℓ is −n and not 0","w":["λ̂ is the largest count, and λ̂ = x̄ for gaps; when every count is 0 there is no best guess","λ̂ = k̄ and λ̂ = 1/x̄; when every count is 0 the slope of ℓ is 0 at λ̂ = 0","λ̂ = 1/k̄ and λ̂ = x̄; when every count is 0 the best guess is λ̂ = 1/n"],"why":"A top at the end of the range is found by looking at the end, not by setting the slope to 0: the end-guess technique of 14·stat, for counts."},
 {"u":"30r3.1","q":"What does the bell-guess technique give for μ and σ², and how does its guess of the spread differ from S²?","a":"μ̂ = x̄ and v̂ = the average squared miss from x̄, with n in the bottom; its average is (n − 1)σ²/n, a little low, where S² with n − 1 averages σ² exactly","w":["μ̂ = x̄ and v̂ = S², the same as 30q2","μ̂ = the middle reading and v̂ with n − 1 in the bottom; it averages σ²","μ̂ = x̄ and v̂ with n + 1 in the bottom; it averages a little high"],"why":"The best guess by likelihood need not be right on average."},
 {"u":"30r4.1","q":"What does the top-bend technique say the number 1/(−ℓ″(θ̂)) is, and what is held in trust about θ̂ for large samples?","a":"it is the spread of the best guess θ̂: a sharply bent top is a narrow guess; held in trust is that for large samples θ̂ is spread nearly as a bell about the true θ with that spread","w":["it is the best guess itself; held in trust is that θ̂ equals θ for large samples","it is the chance that θ̂ is right; held in trust is that this chance heads to 1","it is the width of the sample; held in trust is that θ̂ is spread evenly for large samples"],"why":"For a coin, 1/(−ℓ″(p̂)) = p̂(1 − p̂)/n."},
 {"u":"30r5.1","q":"Why is “L(0.7) ≈ 0.267, so the chance that p = 0.7 is 0.267” a move to watch out for?","a":"0.267 is the chance of the seen sample if p = 0.7; a likelihood is not a chance of p, and summed over all p it need not come to 1","w":["the chance that p = 0.7 is 1 − 0.267","0.267 is the chance that p = 0.7 only when the sample is large","nothing is wrong; the likelihood is the chance of the value"],"why":"A chance for p needs a belief before the sample (30u)."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["30s"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["30s"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"30s1.1","q":"What is the surprise chance of a seen test number z, and why is not rejecting a claim not proving it?","a":"the chance, if the claim is true, of a test number at least as far out as z; a number that is not far out fits the claim, and fits many nearby claims as well, so the test has only failed to refute","w":["the chance that the claim is true, given z; not rejecting shows the claim is probably true","the chance of exactly z if the claim is true; not rejecting proves the claim at level 0.05","the chance that the claim is false, given z; not rejecting shows the sample was too large"],"why":"A small sample rejects almost nothing."},
 {"u":"30s2.1","q":"How is the small-sample-test technique the small-sample-cover technique turned around, and what spread does the two-sample technique give X̄₁ − X̄₂?","a":"reject μ = μ₀ just when μ₀ lies outside the 95% interval, which gives a level of exactly 0.05; the spread of the difference is σ₁²/n₁ + σ₂²/n₂","w":["reject μ = μ₀ just when μ₀ lies inside the interval; the spread of the difference is σ₁²/n₁ − σ₂²/n₂","reject when x̄ lies outside the interval; the spread of the difference is (σ₁ + σ₂)²/(n₁ + n₂)","reject μ = μ₀ when μ₀ lies outside the interval, at a level of about 0.95; the spread is σ₁² + σ₂²"],"why":"Spreads of independent averages add, even under a minus sign."},
 {"u":"30s3.1","q":"What are the two kinds of error, what is power, and what alone buys power at a fixed level?","a":"rejecting a true claim, and keeping a false one; power is the chance of rejecting when the claim is false; at a fixed level only a larger sample buys it, and the cost goes as the square: half the difference to find, four times the sample","w":["rejecting a true claim, and rejecting a false one; power is the level; a smaller sample buys it","keeping a true claim, and keeping a false one; power is 1 minus the level; a looser level alone buys it","rejecting a true claim, and keeping a false one; power is bought by a larger sample at a cost that goes as the difference: half the difference, twice the sample"],"why":"The width of X̄ falls only as 1/√n."},
 {"u":"30s4.1","q":"What is the test number X², and what does the two-cell technique say about it?","a":"X² is the sum over the cells of (seen − expected)²/expected; with two cells X² = Z², the square of the bell test number, so one cell is free: one degree","w":["X² is the sum of (seen − expected)/expected; with two cells it is 2Z","X² is the sum of (seen − expected)² over the cells; with two cells it has two degrees","X² is the largest (seen − expected)²/expected; with two cells it equals Z"],"why":"c cells have c − 1 degrees; a table has (r − 1)(k − 1)."},
 {"u":"30s5.1","q":"Why is “surprise chance 0.18, so the chance that the coin is fair is 0.18” a move to watch out for?","a":"0.18 is the chance of tosses this far out if the coin is fair; it is worked out with the claim taken as true, so it cannot be a chance of the claim","w":["the chance that the coin is fair is 1 − 0.18","0.18 is the chance the coin is fair only for a two-sided test","nothing is wrong; a surprise chance is the chance of the claim"],"why":"P(data given claim) is not P(claim given data)."});
